From 9725ac50794b7028165a6b2e3dd4cd1a22f2b908 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:59:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: a `#` comments only at a word
 start; a quote or backslash in a comment opens nothing

bash reads `a#b` as one word, but `_shlex_tokens` let shlex treat every unquoted `#` as a
comment and dropped the rest of the line, so `echo a#b; bash ./r.sh` ran a lifecycle script the
guard never opened. `_split_logical_lines` ignored comments, so the apostrophe in
`echo hi # it's` opened a "quote" that swallowed the next lines, which the tokenizer then cut at
that comment: `echo hi # it's<newline>bash ./r.sh` was allowed. Found reviewing PR #2523
(t_01a1d6db4b966c37); main gives the same verdicts (card t_01a1d6e0355ac8cc).

Two more shapes of the same root cause, found while fixing it:
- A comment's trailing backslash continues nothing in bash, but the continuation collapse
  (`_SHELL_LINE_CONTINUATION`, and `.replace("\\\n", "")` before the line split) joined the
  next line into the comment: `grep x /dev/null # x \<newline><restart>` was allowed.
- The line splitter honoured a backslash inside single quotes, so `echo 'a\'<newline>bash ./r.sh`
  stayed "quoted" across the newline.

Fix (cron/lifecycle_guard_lines.py): one quote/escape/comment scanner where `#` is a comment only
after the line start, unquoted whitespace or `;&|()<>`, and a backslash escapes nothing in single
quotes. `strip_shell_comments` feeds `_shlex_tokens` (commenters now empty) and the logical-line
split; `join_line_continuations` replaces `_SHELL_LINE_CONTINUATION.sub(" ", ...)` at its three
callers, keeping a comment's newline and joining `a\<newline>#b` into `a#b` as bash does.

Tests: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_comment_words.py. Each RUN row first runs in real bash
with a fake `hermes` that writes a marker. On origin/main 147a9e9d51, 24 of 26 RUN rows and 3 of
8 split rows fail (the other 2 RUN rows already pass through shlex's per-physical-line fallback
and stay as pins); all 59 pass here, as do the clean twins (`echo a#b; python3 ./clean.py`) and
real comments (`echo a#b # bash ./r.sh`). The 30 test files importing the guard pass (1956 tests).
The `{fd}>` shapes from the card (`x=1#2 {fd}>/dev/null bash r.sh`) also need PR #2523.
